  labs:[
    {id:'document',name:'Document lab',kicker:'READ BETWEEN THE LINES',title:'The exception changes the answer.',task:'Summarise the policy in 100 words. List staff actions, risks and missing information. Answer: Does every purchase below $2,000 require two quotes?',raw:'FICTIONAL POLICY · Harbour Services · Purchasing v0.8\nDraft for workshop use, not an approved organisational policy.\n\n1. Routine purchases below $2,000 require two written supplier quotes and supervisor approval before ordering.\n2. Purchases of $2,000 or more require finance approval and three written quotes.\n3. Emergency repairs posing an immediate safety risk may proceed with one quote. The duty manager must record the reason and notify finance within one working day.\n4. Staff must declare supplier conflicts of interest.\n5. Quotes and approvals must be stored in the purchasing register.\n6. Review date: not assigned. Tax treatment, currency and the approval route when the duty manager is absent are not specified.',questions:['Which exception changes the summary?','What approval information is missing?'],reveal:'No. The emergency safety-repair exception permits one quote with a recorded reason and finance notification within one working day. The document is a draft. Confirm currency, tax treatment, review date and substitute approval authority.'},
    {id:'research',name:'Research lab',kicker:'ASK · INVESTIGATE · VERIFY · REFINE',title:'A confident claim needs a source.',task:'Research: Under what conditions might a four-day workweek improve service-team productivity without reducing customer coverage? Find two relevant primary sources. Check dates, sample, limitations and relevance before recommending a pilot.',raw:'Fictional starting claim: “Every organisation moving to a four-day week increases productivity by 40%.” No source supplied.\n\nASK: Define productivity and service coverage.\nINVESTIGATE: Look for original studies or trial reports.\nVERIFY: Open each source. Confirm the claim, sample, comparison and limitations.\nREFINE: Narrow the recommendation to the evidence and your service context. If browsing is unavailable, record an evidence plan and mark conclusions unverified.',questions:['Does the original source support the claim?','Would the study apply to a public-facing service?'],reveal:'Reject the universal claim unless credible evidence supports the full scope. Two sources alone do not establish causation. Look for comparison groups, self-selection, reporting bias and changes to work practices. Propose local measures for coverage, quality and workload.'},
    {id:'data',name:'Data lab',kicker:'PATTERNS ARE QUESTIONS',title:'What happened in week three?',task:'Identify two trends, one anomaly, three management questions and one limitation. Show calculations. Avoid claiming a cause from four rows.',raw:'Fictional weekly service activity. Received and closed counts describe events during each week, not the same group of requests. Week 3 received includes 30 duplicates; correction is pending. Staff hours include training. Satisfaction uses voluntary responses.',questions:['What changes after removing the 30 duplicates?','Is “closed ÷ received” a completion rate for the same requests?'],reveal:'Week 3 received is 180 reported, or 150 after the stated duplicate correction. Closed/received is an activity ratio, not a cohort completion rate. Week 4 closes 155 versus 150 received, potentially including older requests. Four weeks do not establish cause or seasonality.'},
    {id:'decision',name:'Decision lab',kicker:'AI SUGGESTS. YOU DECIDE.',title:'Three options. One human decision.',task:'Build evaluation criteria before scoring. Compare coverage, cost, privacy, accessibility and workload. Challenge assumptions and identify missing information. Recommend a next step for a human manager to approve.',raw:'Fictional Seaside Services needs shorter booking queues.\nA: Add Saturday opening. Estimated cost $2,400/month. Staff availability unconfirmed.\nB: Pilot an online booking form. Estimated setup $5,000. Accessibility and privacy review pending. Ongoing cost unknown.\nC: Change the current triage process. Estimated training cost $1,000. Customer impact untested.\nBudget available this quarter: $6,000. Currency unspecified. Target: reduce waiting without excluding customers who need in-person help.',questions:['Which unknown would change your ranking?','Who has authority to approve the choice?'],reveal:'There is no single required option. A small triage pilot or bounded booking trial is defensible if the criteria and assumptions support the choice. Saturday opening needs a recurring cost and staffing assessment. Do not compare monthly and one-off costs as equivalent.'}
  ],
